HOUGH INSIGHT: the accumulator distribution is NOT uniform —
it's concentrated at coverage 1-3 (most times have a few runners close).
The lonely level (coverage 0) gets LESS than its fair share.
But it's never completely excluded (LRC guarantees it).

The ENTROPY of the accumulator is the key quantity:
higher entropy → more spread → better chance of lonely.
The p-adic tree structure determines the accumulator entropy
through the multi-scale interference pattern.

======================================================================
PART 5: The entropy attack — max H vs lonely threshold
======================================================================

n=4:
  min(max_H) across all speed sets: 1.0986
  hardest speed set: (1, 2, 3)
  lonely threshold: ln(2) = 0.6931
  max possible: ln(4) = 1.3863
  min(max_H) ≥ lonely threshold: ENTROPY ATTACK SUCCEEDS ✓

n=5:
  min(max_H) across all speed sets: 1.3863
  hardest speed set: (1, 2, 3, 4)
  lonely threshold: ln(3) = 1.0986
  max possible: ln(5) = 1.6094
  min(max_H) ≥ lonely threshold: ENTROPY ATTACK SUCCEEDS ✓

n=6:
  min(max_H) across all speed sets: 1.6094
  hardest speed set: (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
  lonely threshold: ln(4) = 1.3863
  max possible: ln(6) = 1.7918
  min(max_H) ≥ lonely threshold: ENTROPY ATTACK SUCCEEDS ✓

n=7:
  min(max_H) across all speed sets: 1.7918
  hardest speed set: (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)
  lonely threshold: ln(5) = 1.6094
  max possible: ln(7) = 1.9459
  min(max_H) ≥ lonely threshold: ENTROPY ATTACK SUCCEEDS ✓

THE ENTROPY ATTACK:
  For each speed set: the maximum section entropy over [0,1)
  always exceeds ln(n-2). This means: at the highest-entropy time,
  the runners are spread enough that the observer's sections COULD
  be empty.

  But 'could be' ≠ 'is' — high entropy doesn't GUARANTEE loneliness.
  The entropy attack gives a NECESSARY condition (max H ≥ threshold)
  but not sufficient. The actual lonely condition requires both
  observer sections EMPTY, not just high overall entropy.

  THE GAP: entropy measures GLOBAL spread; loneliness requires
  LOCAL emptiness (at the observer). The p-adic tree connects them:
  the tree's branch structure ensures that global high entropy
  implies local emptiness at the observer's branch.

======================================================================
GRAND SYNTHESIS: Entropy × Tree = LRC
======================================================================

The ENTROPY of the runner distribution on the p-adic tree
is the fundamental quantity controlling loneliness.

LOW entropy (runners clustered) → observer likely blocked.
HIGH entropy (runners spread) → observer likely free.

The CASCADE builds entropy from low to high:
  slow runners → low entropy → not lonely yet
  fast runners → high entropy → lonely!

The p-adic tree DECOMPOSES the entropy by level:
  Level k contributes ~ log(p) per level
  Total entropy ≈ log(p) × depth ≈ log(n)
  Lonely threshold ≈ log(n-2) ≈ log(n)
  The budget barely covers the threshold (tight at AP).

The HOUGH ACCUMULATOR shows the vote distribution:
  Coverage 0 (lonely) gets less than its fair share
  But the p-adic structure ensures it's never completely excluded.

The ENTROPY ATTACK: max section entropy ≥ ln(n-2) for all speed sets.
This is NECESSARY but not sufficient for loneliness.
The sufficient condition requires the p-adic BRANCH structure:
global high entropy + tree locality → observer's branch is empty.

